 \section{Discussion}
-The studies distinguish three questions that a single detection score cannot answer: whether unauthorized motion is detected, whether its physical effect persists, and whether the chosen clearance rule can recognize normal motion. The candidate's reduced benign alarm count initially appears favorable, but two plants have an unreachable clearing condition. A matched ordinary control removes this obstruction and is stronger on the recorded outcomes. The uncertainty-addition component consequently has no demonstrated benefit here.
-
-Improved commissioning addresses one part of the problem. Longer ARX histories reduce normal prediction error and make Q2's condition reachable. The remaining failure on Q3, together with the perfect-reference calculation, shows that reference bias and measurement noise require separate treatment. A maximum of noisy residuals is not interchangeable with a bound on model error. Once it is added to another noisy residual, the available tolerance can be consumed before any genuine physical deviation is considered.
-
-This suggests a practical design sequence: define the physical return target and trusted information; check the return condition under normal motion; then evaluate detection, persistence and recovery jointly. Algebraic reachability, point feasibility and successful dwell probes provide different evidence. None alone establishes a safe reset policy. Statistical estimation, averaging and noise-aware observers are established tools for revising such a decision, and a replacement would need comparison against those tools rather than a new name for the present combination.
-
-Study III makes this distinction quantitative. A windowed uncertainty control can certify normal residuals that an instantaneous control cannot, while still failing under correlated noise or faster within-tolerance variation. The limiting terms have different meanings: a noise term shrinks with effective sample size, whereas the current-state drift term increases with window length. Ignoring correlation improves some reported certificate fractions by weakening the assumption contract, not by demonstrating more reliable recovery. Useful deployment would require these uncertainty quantities to be justified from commissioning observations and plant knowledge; they were supplied by the generator in Study III.
-
-The same-plant counterfactual also changes interpretation. In 21 intervention episodes, nominal-reference and actual-plant return times disagree. Measuring recovery against the detector's own nominal model can conflate model mismatch with remaining attack effects. Simulation permits the two trajectories to be separated exactly within the model. In a physical deployment, the counterfactual would itself be uncertain and would need independent justification.
+The central failure was easy to miss if judged only by benign alarm counts. The candidate produced fewer such alarms, yet on two plants its clearing condition could never be satisfied. Removing the uncertainty allowance under the same entry threshold gave better outcomes. In this experiment, the added allowance did not earn its place in the rule. The result also separates questions that an interval detection score combines poorly: whether an attack is detected, whether its physical effect remains, and whether the monitor can recognize recovery.
+
+Better commissioning helps, but only to a point. Longer ARX histories lower normal prediction error and make Q2's clearing condition reachable. Q3 still fails. The perfect-reference calculation explains why fitting alone is insufficient: the empirical maximum contains measurement noise as well as model error. Adding that maximum to another noisy residual can use up the tolerance even when there is no physical deviation. Reference bias and measurement noise therefore need separate treatment.
+
+A recovery monitor should first specify what return means and which observations it can trust. The next check is whether its clearing condition is reachable during normal motion. Only then is it useful to examine detection, alarm persistence and recovery together. Algebraic reachability, the fraction of feasible observations and successful dwell probes answer different parts of that question; none alone establishes a safe reset policy. Any replacement should also be tested against ordinary statistical estimation, averaging and noise-aware observers, since these already address parts of the problem.
+
+Study III shows what averaging can and cannot repair. Windowed bounds certify some normal residuals that the instantaneous bound cannot certify. With stronger correlation or faster variation within tolerance, that advantage disappears. A longer window reduces the noise term but increases the allowance for change between the window average and the current state. Assuming independent noise when it is correlated makes some certificate fractions look better while removing their statistical justification. Here the generator supplied the uncertainty parameters; an operational monitor would have to justify them from commissioning observations and knowledge of the plant.
+
+The choice of recovery reference matters as well. Nominal-reference and actual-plant return times disagree in 21 intervention episodes. If the detector's nominal model also defines the evaluation target, model mismatch can be mistaken for a lingering attack effect. The simulation separates these trajectories within the model. A physical deployment would not have that exact counterfactual, so the recovery target would carry uncertainty of its own.
 
 \subsection{Evidence scope}
-The evidence comes from three specified plant configurations in one simulator. Noise seeds repeat a physical condition; they are not independent robot acquisitions. Geometry, damping, lag and noise vary together, preventing causal attribution of a subgroup difference to a single factor. There are no contact-rich tasks, changeovers between manufacturing processes, field attacks or hardware measurements. The observed return tolerance has no established safety interpretation.
-
-The attack comparison contains common-information controls and a restricted recent calibration component, rather than three full recent paper reproductions. The separate IMU study adds executable, common-input architectural comparisons, but its prefix is reused normal development, its compact models remain epoch-limited, and the exact original publication-run correspondence is unresolved. External-IMU baselines cannot be ranked fairly against protected-command monitors without resolving their input and protocol differences. This limits comparative-performance claims even where the internal ablations are informative.
-
-The stages have different evidential roles. Study I prospectively evaluates a fixed candidate. Study II diagnoses its failure on new normal tasks. The post-study calculation explains a mechanism under explicit assumptions. Study III was specified before generating its residual traces, but it was motivated by the earlier failures and remains a simplified diagnostic. Its three noise realizations are paired across settings and are not independent robot acquisitions. All reported conditions are now exposed. A redesigned rule evaluated on them would be exploratory until tested under a new prospective protocol.
+The simulated motion comes from three plant configurations in one simulator. Repeating them with different noise seeds does not provide independent robot acquisitions. Geometry, damping, lag and noise also vary together, so a subgroup difference cannot be attributed to one of those factors alone. The study covers neither contact-rich tasks nor manufacturing-process changeovers, field attacks or hardware measurements. The chosen return tolerance has no established safety interpretation.
+
